WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.453
Computer architecture becomes easier
when we separate what software is promised

2
00:00:04.453 --> 00:00:06.655
from how hardware keeps that promise.

3
00:00:06.655 --> 00:00:11.373
The RISC-V instruction set defines
what an ADD instruction means and how its

4
00:00:11.373 --> 00:00:12.384
fields are encoded.

5
00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:17.651
A classic five-stage pipeline is one
microarchitecture that can implement that

6
00:00:17.651 --> 00:00:18.090
contract.

7
00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:24.212
We will trace one RV32I instruction:
add x5, x6, x7.

8
00:00:24.314 --> 00:00:29.929
In this RV32I example, the
instruction word is hexadecimal zero zero

9
00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:32.736
seven three zero two B three.

10
00:00:32.736 --> 00:00:38.291
The opcode, funct three, and funct
seven fields select register-register ADD.

11
00:00:38.291 --> 00:00:41.555
The source fields name x6 and x7.

12
00:00:41.555 --> 00:00:44.738
The destination field names x5.

13
00:00:44.738 --> 00:00:50.132
Decode is not guessing from assembly
text; the hardware receives these exact bits.

14
00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:56.481
Assume the program counter is zero x
one thousand and this teaching example uses

15
00:00:56.481 --> 00:00:59.396
32-bit RV32I instructions without the
compressed extension.

16
00:00:59.396 --> 00:01:04.389
Fetch uses that address to read the
instruction word, while the sequential next

17
00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:06.885
address becomes zero x one thousand four.

18
00:01:06.885 --> 00:01:11.843
Decode separates the fields,
generates control signals, and asks the

19
00:01:11.843 --> 00:01:14.547
register file for x6 and x7.

20
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:19.546
Suppose x6 contains nineteen and x7
contains twenty-three.

21
00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:24.566
In Execute, operand-select logic
sends both register values to the arithmetic

22
00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:25.403
logic unit.

23
00:01:25.403 --> 00:01:29.627
The ADD control selects integer
addition, producing forty-two.

24
00:01:29.627 --> 00:01:34.940
For RV32I, ADD keeps the low
thirty-two bits of the result.

25
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:38.111
Arithmetic overflow does not raise
an exception.

26
00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:41.308
ADD does not need a data-memory read
or write.

27
00:01:41.308 --> 00:01:45.612
In this five-stage teaching
processor, the value still advances through

28
00:01:45.612 --> 00:01:49.525
the pipeline registers that separate Execute,
Memory, and Write Back.

29
00:01:49.525 --> 00:01:54.173
Those registers keep the result,
destination number, and write-enable control

30
00:01:54.173 --> 00:01:54.595
aligned.

31
00:01:54.595 --> 00:01:58.553
At Write Back, the processor commits
forty-two into x5.

32
00:01:58.658 --> 00:02:01.420
The payoff appears when more
instructions follow.

33
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.922
While ADD is in Execute, a second
independent instruction can be in Decode and a

34
00:02:06.922 --> 00:02:08.642
third can be in Fetch.

35
00:02:08.642 --> 00:02:13.316
After the pipeline fills, this
idealized sequence can complete one

36
00:02:13.316 --> 00:02:14.591
instruction per cycle.

37
00:02:14.591 --> 00:02:16.119
That improves throughput.

38
00:02:16.119 --> 00:02:21.073
It does not mean one instruction
suddenly crosses all five stages in one cycle.

39
00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.470
This clean timing chart assumes
independent instructions and available

40
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:25.900
hardware.

41
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:31.085
If the next instruction needs x5
before normal writeback, the processor has a

42
00:02:31.085 --> 00:02:31.826
data hazard.

43
00:02:31.826 --> 00:02:34.580
It may forward the result or insert
a bubble.

44
00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.372
Branches create control hazards
because already-fetched work may be wrong.

45
00:02:39.372 --> 00:02:44.918
Real pipelines add detection and
recovery logic, but the governing rule stays

46
00:02:44.918 --> 00:02:48.758
simple: preserve the ISA result first, then
optimize throughput.
